  "content": {
    "id": "p39-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "",
    "prompt": "Some more complex questions may require you to divide by a quadratic divisor. Look at the example and complete the divisions.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p39-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$(x^3-3x^2+3x-1)\\div(x^2+5)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x-3+\\dfrac{-2x+14}{x^2+5}$",
          "worked": "Divide the leading terms: $x^3\\div x^2=x$. Multiply and subtract:\n$$\\begin{aligned}(x^3-3x^2+3x-1)-x(x^2+5)&=-3x^2-2x-1.\\end{aligned}$$\nNext, $-3x^2\\div x^2=-3$. Multiply and subtract:\n$$(-3x^2-2x-1)-(-3)(x^2+5)=-2x+14.$$\nThe remainder has degree less than 2, so\n$$\\frac{x^3-3x^2+3x-1}{x^2+5}=x-3+\\frac{-2x+14}{x^2+5}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$(2x^3+4x^2-x+8)\\div(x^2+3x+2)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2x-2+\\dfrac{x+12}{x^2+3x+2}$",
          "worked": "Divide the leading terms: $2x^3\\div x^2=2x$. Multiply and subtract:\n$$(2x^3+4x^2-x+8)-2x(x^2+3x+2)=-2x^2-5x+8.$$\nNext, $-2x^2\\div x^2=-2$. Multiply and subtract:\n$$(-2x^2-5x+8)-(-2)(x^2+3x+2)=x+12.$$\nThe remainder has degree less than 2, so\n$$\\frac{2x^3+4x^2-x+8}{x^2+3x+2}=2x-2+\\frac{x+12}{x^2+3x+2}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 65
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q5-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$(2x^4-5x^3+2x^2+2x-5)\\div(x^2-2x)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2x^2-x+\\dfrac{2x-5}{x^2-2x}$",
          "worked": "Divide the leading terms: $2x^4\\div x^2=2x^2$. Multiply and subtract:\n$$(2x^4-5x^3+2x^2+2x-5)-2x^2(x^2-2x)=-x^3+2x^2+2x-5.$$\nNext, $-x^3\\div x^2=-x$. Multiply and subtract:\n$$(-x^3+2x^2+2x-5)-(-x)(x^2-2x)=2x-5.$$\nThe remainder has degree less than 2, so\n$$\\frac{2x^4-5x^3+2x^2+2x-5}{x^2-2x}=2x^2-x+\\frac{2x-5}{x^2-2x}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q5-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$(x^5+x^3+5x^2-6x+15)\\div(x^2+3)$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x^3-2x+5$",
          "worked": "Write the dividend as $x^5+0x^4+x^3+5x^2-6x+15$ to keep powers aligned. Divide the leading terms: $x^5\\div x^2=x^3$. Multiply and subtract:\n$$(x^5+x^3+5x^2-6x+15)-x^3(x^2+3)=-2x^3+5x^2-6x+15.$$\nNext, $-2x^3\\div x^2=-2x$. Multiply and subtract:\n$$(-2x^3+5x^2-6x+15)-(-2x)(x^2+3)=5x^2+15.$$\nFinally, $5x^2\\div x^2=5$, and\n$$(5x^2+15)-5(x^2+3)=0.$$\nThe remainder is zero, so\n$$\\frac{x^5+x^3+5x^2-6x+15}{x^2+3}=x^3-2x+5.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 82
      }
    ]
  },
